 {id:"QR-003824",title:"Which finding best indicates that immediate supportive nursing care is maintaining safety while definitive treatment decisions are made?",options:["The client remains NPO, has a patent airway, and has no new decline in oxygenation or level of consciousness.","The client drinks water without a formal swallowing assessment.","The nurse delays repeat neurologic checks until the end of the shift.","The blood pressure is rapidly reduced without an order."],correctAnswer:"The client remains NPO, has a patent airway, and has no new decline in oxygenation or level of consciousness.",rationale:"Maintaining airway safety, withholding oral intake until swallowing is assessed, and monitoring for neurologic or respiratory deterioration are appropriate supportive measures. These findings do not prove that the stroke has resolved; ongoing reassessment and stroke-team management remain essential.",optionRationales:{"The client remains NPO, has a patent airway, and has no new decline in oxygenation or level of consciousness.":"Correct. These findings support immediate safety while diagnostic evaluation and treatment planning continue.","The client drinks water without a formal swallowing assessment.":"Unsafe because dysphagia may be present and aspiration can occur silently.","The nurse delays repeat neurologic checks until the end of the shift.":"Unsafe; acute neurologic status requires frequent reassessment according to the stroke protocol.","The blood pressure is rapidly reduced without an order.":"Unsafe; blood-pressure targets and treatment depend on stroke type and reperfusion plan."}}
